# วางบล็อกนี้ใน Caddyfile ของโฮสต์ (Caddy รันบนโฮสต์ ไม่ได้อยู่ใน compose)
ledger.tapestopnight.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
}

# The public info pages, the admin config UI, and the Chat Bridge.
#
# The apex is Cloudflare-PROXIED, unlike ledger above. Minecraft reaches the
# origin through the DNS-only mc. record plus an SRV record (ADR-0001), so the
# website does not have to give up Cloudflare's TLS/CDN/DDoS to let the game
# through. Both use one domain.
tapestopnight.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# ── ADR-0016: the visitor's real address ────────────────────────────────
	#
	# Account linking matches the address a browser arrives from against the
	# address that player logged into the game from, so the web app has to know
	# which address is genuinely the visitor's. Without this block it does not:
	# every request reaches Caddy from a Cloudflare edge node, so the peer
	# address is Cloudflare's and X-Forwarded-For's first entry is whatever the
	# client chose to send — which anyone could set to a neighbour's address and
	# be handed their identity.
	#
	# `trusted_proxies` is what makes CF-Connecting-IP believable: Caddy honours
	# that header ONLY when the request actually came from one of these ranges.
	#
	# The list is Cloudflare's own, fetched 2026-09-10 from
	# https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and /ips-v6. It CHANGES. Re-fetch it
	# rather than copying this block forward, and do not type it from memory.
	trusted_proxies static \
		173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 \
		141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 \
		197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 \
		104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 \
		2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 \
		2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
	client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3002 {
		# SET, not add. `header_up` replaces the header, so a client that sends
		# its own X-Real-Client-IP has it overwritten before the app ever sees
		# it. The app reads this one header and no other, and treats its absence
		# as "no match" rather than falling back to something weaker.
		header_up X-Real-Client-IP {client_ip}
	}
}
